  "transcript": "# Regulatory sandboxes in financial services\n\n## What sandboxes are, and the different types\n> There isn't just one sandbox—products, tech and regulator-focused sandboxes exist and each serves different aims.\n\nChair: Could you briefly introduce yourselves and tell us whether regulatory sandboxes matter?\nLeo Ringer: I run a VC fund, Form, that backs very early-stage fintechs in regulated markets—mostly pre-authorisation.\nMichael Moore: I'm CEO of UK Private Capital, representing venture capital, private equity and private credit firms.\nDr Andrew Henderson: I'm a partner at Goodwin, a financial services regulatory lawyer advising founders and investors for 25 years.\nDr Andrew Henderson: From my advisory view, sandboxes are useful: they let firms test technology and their regulatory understanding in a controlled way.\nMichael Moore: There are several kinds of sandboxes—testing new rules, giving firms a safe place to experiment, and helping regulators test policy. Each has value.\nLeo Ringer: Early FCA sandboxes were cohort-based and signalled quality. Now there are thematic sandboxes—digital securities, stablecoin—and they serve different purposes.\n\n\n## How investors and startups actually use sandboxes\n> Investors value any early signal that a regulator has vetted a firm, but most early startups choose not to enter sandboxes because of cost and limited payoff.\n\nChair: If sandboxes are important, why don't all fintechs go through them?\nMichael Moore: Startups must choose carefully how they spend time and money. Sandboxes can help, but not every firm gains enough to justify the cost.\nLeo Ringer: When we back a startup the main question is: can they get authorised within our funding window? Early sandboxes gave a positive signal to investors that a firm was regulator-ready.\nLeo Ringer: Since the FCA moved away from six-month cohorts to an open model, the signalling value fell. That matters to VCs deciding whether to invest.\nDr Andrew Henderson: Some clients who are confident simply go straight for authorisation rather than take sandbox 'training wheels'. The FCA also offers lighter-touch help outside the formal sandbox.\nLord Eatwell: Some witnesses said sandboxes now favour big firms that can afford them and that small disruptors are disadvantaged. Is that fair?\nLeo Ringer: Partly. Big incumbents can afford sandbox tests. For very small challengers, the time and cost of applying sometimes outweigh the benefits.\nDr Andrew Henderson: It depends on the business. Some prefer to go direct to authorisation; others value the early engagement the sandbox can bring.\nMichael Moore: Be cautious: sandboxes should not become a longer, slower route to authorisation that swallows startups' time and cash.\nLeo Ringer: Startups often misunderstand what a sandbox achieves. It rarely gives a free pass; you usually still need authorisation and the liability issues remain.\nDr Andrew Henderson: In some cases firms were authorised with limitations during testing and had those lifted later. But outcomes vary a lot by the business and the facts.\nMichael Moore: For investors, the key is whether the UK regulatory environment remains competitive and predictable so capital keeps being deployed here.\n\n\n## Limits, misunderstandings and what needs fixing\n> Sandboxes often don't give authorisation, can be resource-intensive, and their learning isn't well connected into rule change—so reforms are needed.\n\nLord Lilley: Why have a sandbox if it doesn't grant authorisation and just adds cost and delay?\nLeo Ringer: A common misconception is that sandbox entry equals easier authorisation. Usually you still need authorisation; the sandbox can offer limited permissions but rarely full, permanent ones.\nDr Andrew Henderson: Sometimes firms get temporary limitations lifted after testing. The sandbox can be helpful where rules don't fit new tech, but it's fact-specific.\nMichael Moore: The danger is turning innovation into extra bureaucracy. Sandboxes must not become a substitute for clear regulatory decisions where they're needed.\nLeo Ringer: If sandboxes are to matter more they must feed learning into policy teams so rules can be rewritten where necessary—otherwise they are just an information funnel.\nBaroness Donaghy: Do firms still enter sandboxes mainly for kudos, or has that faded?\nDr Andrew Henderson: Less so now. Firms come to us when there's a real use case; many are better off going straight to authorisation or using informal FCA engagement.\nLeo Ringer: Thematic sandboxes can still create a 'club' effect—for example, being in the stablecoin sandbox can make investors feel a UK strategy is credible.\nLeo Ringer: Three main blockers: regulator resources and technical skills, the regulator's risk appetite, and the difficulty of changing primary law or rules quickly.\nMichael Moore: Ministers and Parliament need to decide how much risk they will tolerate for innovation, and give regulators both permission and cover to take sensible risks.\nDr Andrew Henderson: Legal advice remains important because the rules are often unclear; clients want judgement as well as black-letter law on how to present proposals to the regulator.\nLeo Ringer: Also, don't label everything a 'sandbox'. Be clearer about product sandboxes, technology sandboxes and regulator-led policy sandboxes.\n\n\n## International practice and practical recommendations\n> Other countries show clearer, faster rule change and joined-up action—lessons for the UK include clearer sandbox types, provisional authorisation and more resources.\n\nLord Smith of Kelvin: Can the UK learn from other jurisdictions about doing regulatory innovation better?\nLeo Ringer: Yes. Estonia actively changes rules to enable new activity and has teams that push through law changes; Singapore has iterated the FCA model and linked it closer to rule change.\nDr Andrew Henderson: Smaller or less democratic regimes can act faster, which helps early-stage markets. But London still has advantages in scale and infrastructure.\nMichael Moore: We have strong capital, talent and regulators. The job is to keep the UK competitive by making the regulatory pipeline clear and proportionate.\nLeo Ringer: Practical proposals: pare back 'sandbox' terminology, create provisional authorisation, resource regulators with specialist talent, and get ministerial backing for tolerable failure.\nLord Davies of Brixton: Do sandboxes actually produce innovation that wouldn't otherwise exist?\nLeo Ringer: Sometimes. For technologies that don't fit existing rules—blockchain, lab-grown foods—a focused regulatory approach can keep that activity in the UK rather than losing it abroad.\nMichael Moore: Fix the pipeline—clarify sandbox types, connect learning to policy, and fund specialist skills—and we'll stay competitive internationally.\nDr Andrew Henderson: Sandboxes are useful but not a panacea; legal clarity and better policy channels matter as much as testing environments.\nLeo Ringer: Keep sandboxes, but make them clearer and leaner within a bigger regulatory toolkit—and be honest about resources and tolerable risk.\n",
